Provision Persistent Data Volumes (Kubernetes / OpenShift)
Objective
Provide applications with persistent storage so that data is not lost when pods are deleted.
________________________________________
1. Problem: Container Storage is Ephemeral
By default, containers use ephemeral storage.
Ephemeral Storage
•	Temporary storage
•	Exists only while the pod is running
If the pod is deleted:
Pod Deleted
   ↓
Container Deleted
   ↓
All Files Lost
Example:
Pod writes data → /var/lib/mysql
Pod crashes → new pod created
Old data lost
This is why Kubernetes provides Persistent Storage.
________________________________________
2. Kubernetes Persistent Storage Architecture
Main components:
Application
     ↓
Persistent Volume Claim (PVC)
     ↓
Persistent Volume (PV)
     ↓
Storage System (NFS, AWS EBS, etc.)
Explanation
Component	Role
PV	actual storage in cluster
PVC	request for storage
StorageClass	storage type definition
________________________________________
3. Two Ways to Provision Storage
1️⃣ Static Provisioning
Administrator manually creates Persistent Volumes.
Flow:
Admin → Create PV
Developer → Create PVC
PVC → binds to PV
Example:
PV: 100Gi
PVC request: 50Gi
PVC binds to PV
________________________________________
2️⃣ Dynamic Provisioning
Storage is created automatically using StorageClass.
Flow:
Developer creates PVC
        ↓
StorageClass creates PV automatically
        ↓
PVC binds to new PV
Dynamic provisioning is most common in production.
________________________________________
4. Storage Classes
What is StorageClass?
A StorageClass defines the type of storage.
Example types:
•	SSD storage
•	HDD storage
•	NFS storage
•	Cloud storage
Example:
nfs-storage
aws-ebs
ceph
lvms
Command to view:
oc get storageclass
Example output:
NAME           PROVISIONER
nfs-storage    k8s-sigs.io/nfs
lvms-vg1       topolvm.io
Default storage class is marked:
(default)
________________________________________
5. Persistent Volume (PV)
What is PV?
A Persistent Volume is actual storage in the cluster.
It could be:
•	NFS server
•	Cloud disk
•	Storage array
•	Local disk
________________________________________
Example PV Manifest
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: block-pv
spec:
  capacity:
    storage: 10Gi
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  volumeMode: Block
  persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Retain
  fc:
    targetWWNs: ["50060e801049cfd1"]
    lun: 0
    readOnly: false
________________________________________
Explanation
Field	Meaning
kind	PersistentVolume resource
capacity	storage size
accessModes	access permissions
volumeMode	filesystem or block
reclaimPolicy	what happens after delete
fc	fiber channel storage
________________________________________
Create PV
oc create -f my-pv.yaml
This command creates a PersistentVolume object.
________________________________________
6. Persistent Volume Claim (PVC)
What is PVC?
A PVC is a storage request by an application.
Developers request:
•	storage size
•	access mode
•	storage class
They do NOT need to know storage infrastructure.
________________________________________
PVC Example
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: mypvc
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 15Gi
________________________________________
Explanation
Field	Meaning
kind	PVC resource
name	claim name
accessModes	storage access type
storage	requested size
________________________________________
Create PVC
oc create -f pvc.yaml
________________________________________
View PVC
oc get pvc
Example:
NAME   STATUS   VOLUME     CAPACITY
mypvc  Bound    pvc-1234   15Gi
Status Meaning
Status	Meaning
Pending	waiting for PV
Bound	PVC connected to PV
________________________________________
7. Access Modes
Access modes define how pods access storage.
Mode	Meaning
ReadWriteOnce (RWO)	one node read/write
ReadWriteOncePod (RWOP)	single pod
ReadOnlyMany (ROX)	many nodes read-only
ReadWriteMany (RWX)	many nodes read/write
________________________________________
Example
Storage	Access
Local disk	RWO
NFS	RWX
Cloud disk	RWO
________________________________________
8. Volume Types
Common volume types:
Volume	Description
configMap	configuration data
emptyDir	temporary storage
hostPath	host machine directory
iSCSI	block storage
local	node disk
NFS	shared network storage
________________________________________
emptyDir
Temporary storage inside pod.
Example use:
cache
temporary files
logs
Deleted when pod is deleted.
________________________________________
hostPath
Mounts host machine directory.
Example:
/var/data (host)
↓
mounted in pod
⚠ Not recommended in production.
________________________________________
NFS
Network File System.
Features:
Shared storage
Multiple pods access same data
Access mode supported:
RWX
________________________________________
9. Volume Modes
Kubernetes supports two modes:
Mode	Description
Filesystem	normal files
Block	raw disk access
________________________________________
Filesystem Mode
Example:
/data/file.txt
Default mode.
________________________________________
Block Mode
Raw disk device.
Example:
/dev/sdb
Used for:
•	databases
•	high-performance storage
________________________________________
10. Adding PVC to Deployment
Use command:
oc set volumes deployment/example-application \
--add \
--name example-pv-storage \
--type persistentVolumeClaim \
--claim-mode rwo \
--claim-size 15Gi \
--mount-path /var/lib/example-app \
--claim-name mypvc
________________________________________
Command Explanation
Option	Meaning
deployment/example	deployment name
--add	add volume
--name	volume name
--type	pvc volume
--claim-mode	access mode
--claim-size	storage size
--mount-path	container path
--claim-name	pvc name
________________________________________
What This Command Does
It automatically:
1️⃣ Creates PVC
2️⃣ Updates deployment
3️⃣ Mounts storage in container
________________________________________
Deployment after PVC mount
volumeMounts:
- mountPath: /var/lib/example-app
  name: example-pv-storage
and
volumes:
- name: example-pv-storage
  persistentVolumeClaim:
    claimName: mypvc
Meaning:
Container path → /var/lib/example-app
Storage source → mypvc
________________________________________
11. Dynamic Provisioning Example
Command:
oc set volumes deployment/example-application \
--add \
--name example-pv-storage \
--type pvc \
--claim-class nfs-storage \
--claim-mode rwo \
--claim-size 15Gi \
--mount-path /var/lib/example-app \
--claim-name mypvc
________________________________________
What happens here?
PVC created
     ↓
StorageClass (nfs-storage)
     ↓
PV created automatically
     ↓
PVC binds to PV
________________________________________
12. PV Lifecycle
Persistent Volumes follow lifecycle stages.
________________________________________
1️⃣ Available
PV created but not used.
PV ready for claim
________________________________________
2️⃣ Bound
PVC successfully attached to PV.
PVC ↔ PV
________________________________________
3️⃣ In Use
Pods are using the storage.
Pod → PVC → PV
________________________________________
4️⃣ Released
PVC deleted but PV still exists.
PV released
________________________________________
5️⃣ Reclaimed
Cluster decides what to do with storage.
Depends on reclaim policy.
________________________________________
13. Reclaim Policy
Two policies exist.
Policy	Behavior
Retain	keep storage
Delete	delete storage
________________________________________
Retain
Admin must manually clean storage.
Used for:
important data
________________________________________
Delete
Storage deleted automatically.
Used in:
dynamic provisioning
________________________________________
14. Delete PVC
Command:
oc delete pvc/mypvc
Effect:
PVC deleted
PV released
Storage reclaimed
